\\]\n\nMore generally, if utility depends explicitly on the action as well as the state, write\n\n\\[\nV_K(A)=\\sum_s P(s\\mid A,K)\\,u(A,s).\n\\]\n\nThe probabilities are the agent’s credences: they represent uncertainty in the decision problem, conditional on the information available at the time. The utility function represents what the agent values. A monetary payoff can stand in for utility in a simple example, but only if the agent’s preferences make that approximation appropriate.\n\nThe characteristic term is \\(P(s\\mid A,K)\\). To evaluate \\(A\\), the agent asks: *if I learned that I took \\(A\\), what would that tell me about the state?* This is ordinary conditional probability, not necessarily a claim that the action brings the state about.\n\nFor positive-probability actions, Bayes’ rule shows how the evidential effect arises:\n\n\\[\nP(s\\mid A,K)\n=\n\\frac{P(A\\mid s,K)P(s\\mid K)}{P(A\\mid K)}.\n\\]\n\nAn action is evidence for a state when it is more likely to be taken in that state than in relevant alternatives. Such a dependence might arise because the state causes the action, because the action causes something included in the outcome, or because both are consequences of a common factor.\n\nEDT’s rule is sometimes summarized as “do what you most want to learn that you will do.” This is a decision principle, not merely a recommendation to gather information. The agent is not choosing which report to read; it is evaluating which action to take by considering the outcomes associated with learning that it was taken.\n\n## 2. Conditional and causal comparisons\n\nConsider a causal model in which a hidden variable \\(H\\) influences both your action \\(A\\) and an outcome \\(O\\):\n\n\\[\nA \\longleftarrow H \\longrightarrow O.\n\\]\n\nObserving \\(A\\) provides evidence about \\(H\\), and thus about \\(O\\). But forcing \\(A\\) to a particular value while leaving the process generating \\(H\\) unchanged need not change \\(O\\).\n\nIn a suitable structural causal model, the contrast can be written as\n\n\\[\nV_{\\mathrm{EDT}}(a)=\\mathbb E[u(O)\\mid A=a,K],\n\\qquad\nV_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\mathbb E[u(O)\\mid \\operatorname{do}(A=a),K].\n\\]\n\nHere \\(\\operatorname{do}(A=a)\\) represents an intervention that sets the action to \\(a\\). This notation is a useful illustration of CDT’s causal comparison, although not every philosophical account of causal counterfactuals is identical to a particular intervention model.\n\nConditioning preserves the ordinary statistical relationship between action and hidden state. Intervention breaks the causal routes *into* the action, while preserving its effects downstream. Consequently,\n\n\\[\nP(O\\mid A=a,K)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nP(O\\mid \\operatorname{do}(A=a),K)\n\\]\n\ncan differ. When there is no relevant dependency between the action and a state except through the action’s causal effects, the two approaches will often agree.\n\nThis difference is central to EDT’s motivation and to its criticism. Its defenders hold that the association between an action and favorable outcomes is relevant even when it is not causal. Critics ask why an agent should treat good news as though it were a good result to be produced.\n\n## 3. Newcomb’s problem\n\nThe reference article’s principal example is Newcomb’s problem. A predictor has already filled an opaque box \\(B\\), either with nothing or with \\$1,000,000. A transparent box \\(A\\) contains \\$1,000. The predictor filled \\(B\\) if it predicted that you would take only \\(B\\), and left it empty if it predicted that you would take both boxes.\n\nSuppose the relevant conditional probabilities are\n\n\\[\nP(B\\text{ full}\\mid \\text{one-box})=0.99,\n\\qquad\nP(B\\text{ full}\\mid \\text{two-box})=0.01.\n\\]\n\nAssuming utility is proportional to money over this range, EDT calculates\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{one-box})=0.99(\\$1{,}000{,}000)=\\$990{,}000,\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{two-box})\n&=0.01(\\$1{,}001{,}000)+0.99(\\$1{,}000)\\\\\n&=\\$11{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEDT therefore recommends taking only the opaque box. One-boxing is strong evidence that the predictor predicted one-boxing and filled it.\n\nThere is a subtle probabilistic assumption here. Saying merely that a predictor is correct on 99% of occasions does **not**, by itself, establish both conditional probabilities above. Overall accuracy is\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{prediction matches choice})=0.99,\n\\]\n\nwhereas the calculations require accuracy conditional on each particular choice. If one choice is very rare, a predictor can have high overall accuracy while being much less accurate on that choice. The reference’s numerical example works when its displayed conditional probabilities are granted; applying it to a new predictor requires checking what kind of accuracy is actually known.\n\nThe causal argument for taking both boxes is straightforward. The contents of \\(B\\) have already been fixed. Whether \\(B\\) is full or empty, taking both yields \\$1,000 more than taking only \\(B\\). Under the usual causal model, changing the current choice cannot alter the earlier prediction or its consequence. EDT does not deny that temporal fact. It says that the evidentially relevant distribution over the already-fixed contents differs between the two possible choices.\n\nThis is why the disagreement cannot be settled merely by repeating either “the past is fixed” or “the predictor is accurate.” Both statements are compatible with the setup. The dispute concerns which comparison is normatively appropriate.\n\n## 4. Correlated decision-makers\n\nThe same structure can occur without a prediction. Imagine Aomame plays a prisoner’s dilemma against a psychological twin in another room. Their individual payoffs are:\n\n| Aomame | Twin | Aomame’s payoff |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Cooperate | Cooperate | \\$5 |\n| Cooperate | Defect | \\$0 |\n| Defect | Cooperate | \\$10 |\n| Defect | Defect | \\$1 |\n\nIf the twin’s action is held fixed, defecting gives Aomame more money. But suppose their reasoning processes and situations are so closely matched that, conditional on either choice by Aomame, the twin makes the same choice:\n\n\\[\nP(C_{\\text{twin}}\\mid C_{\\text{Aomame}})=1,\n\\qquad\nP(C_{\\text{twin}}\\mid D_{\\text{Aomame}})=0.\n\\]\n\nThen\n\n\\[\nV(C)=1(5)+0(0)=5,\n\\qquad\nV(D)=0(10)+1(1)=1.\n\\]\n\nEDT recommends cooperation, even if Aomame cares only about her own payoff. Her choice is evidence of the twin’s choice; communication and a direct causal influence on the twin are unnecessary.\n\nPerfect correlation is not essential. If\n\n\\[\np=P(C_{\\text{twin}}\\mid C_{\\text{Aomame}}),\n\\quad\nq=P(C_{\\text{twin}}\\mid D_{\\text{Aomame}}),\n\\]\n\nthen\n\n\\[\nV(C)=5p,\\qquad V(D)=10q+1(1-q)=1+9q.\n\\]\n\nCooperation is favored exactly when\n\n\\[\n5p>1+9q.\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of the dependency matters: vague similarity between players is not enough. Nor should one simply set \\(p=1\\) and \\(q=0\\) whenever two people reason similarly. They may have different private information, incentives, or independent randomization devices. The evidential calculation must reflect those differences.\n\n## 5. What goes into a decision model?\n\nFor an unfamiliar problem, the formula is easy to write down; constructing the probabilities is the hard part. A practical procedure is:\n\n1. **Specify the decision and information.** List the available acts and the evidence \\(K\\) the agent actually has. Do not condition on facts the agent has not learned.\n2. **Define outcomes and utilities.** Include direct consequences, side effects, and anything else the agent values. Separate money from utility if marginal utility or risk matters.\n3. **Model dependencies.** Identify hidden variables, predictors, other agents, and causal effects. Ask why the agent’s different acts would be associated with different states.\n4. **Calculate conditional values.** For each act, use the same background model to find \\(P(O_j\\mid A,K)\\), then compute \\(V_K(A)\\).\n5. **Test assumptions.** Check that the conditional probabilities are coherent with the stated accuracy rates, base rates, available information, and means of choosing.\n6. **Compare with causal alternatives when useful.** This reveals whether the verdict depends on evidence about a fixed or independently generated state, rather than on the act’s direct effects.\n\nThe same action can be excellent evidence in one information state and uninformative in another. For example, an action may correlate with a hidden disposition in the population. Once an agent learns the disposition precisely, the action may supply no further evidence about it. EDT is therefore sensitive not just to broad correlations but to what has already been screened off by \\(K\\).\n\nA useful mathematical check is conditional independence. If \\(S\\) is a payoff-relevant state and\n\n\\[\nP(S\\mid A,K)=P(S\\mid K)\n\\]\n\nfor every available action, then the choice itself carries no further evidence about \\(S\\). EDT still accounts for the actions’ different consequences through \\(u(A,S)\\), but it gives no extra weight to an association with \\(S\\).\n\n## 6. The “managing the news” objection\n\nThe reference article reports David Lewis’s objection that EDT can amount to an irrational policy of “managing the news.” A standard way to see the concern is a medical example.\n\nSuppose an inherited condition causes both a strong desire to smoke and an elevated cancer risk. Imagine, for the sake of argument, that smoking itself has no effect on cancer risk, while smoking is pleasurable. A person knows the population correlations but does not know whether they have the condition. If smokers are more likely to carry it, then learning “I smoke” is bad news about cancer. EDT may therefore recommend abstaining, even though abstention cannot remove the inherited condition and sacrifices pleasure.\n\nWith a simple numerical utility scale, suppose\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{condition}\\mid \\text{smoke})=0.8,\\quad\nP(\\text{condition}\\mid \\text{abstain})=0.2.\n\\]\n\nLet the condition cost 10 utility units, smoking provide 1 unit, and abstention provide none. Then\n\n\\[\nV(\\text{smoke})=1-10(0.8)=-7,\n\\qquad\nV(\\text{abstain})=-10(0.2)=-2.\n\\]\n\nEDT chooses abstention. A causal calculation that holds the inherited condition’s distribution fixed would choose smoking, since it adds pleasure without affecting the condition. The criticism is that abstention makes the *news* better without making the underlying condition less likely in the causally relevant sense.\n\nThis example is deliberately artificial: real smoking has major causal health effects. Its purpose is to isolate the philosophical issue, not to give medical advice.\n\nAn evidentialist response may question whether the stipulated probabilities remain correct after accounting for the agent’s introspective evidence. If the agent already knows how strong their desire is, and desire screens off the correlation between choice and condition, then their act may supply little additional evidence. But that response depends on the model: the unwanted verdict is not eliminated merely by saying that the agent has some self-knowledge. One must show that the relevant conditional dependence actually disappears.\n\n## 7. Policies, randomization, and zero-probability acts\n\nThe basic rule evaluates acts, but agents also choose policies: rules mapping information or situations to actions. A policy \\(\\pi\\) can itself be treated as the object of evidential evaluation,\n\n\\[\nV_K(\\pi)=\\sum_o P(o\\mid \\Pi=\\pi,K)\\,u(o),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\Pi\\) is the policy the agent adopts or implements. A policy-level analysis can be useful when a predictor forecasts the agent’s general procedure or when repeated decisions are linked. It also makes clear that “choose a policy” and “choose an act now” are not automatically the same problem: they involve different events, information, and possibly different dependencies.\n\nThe reference article gives the conditional-value rule but does not settle every question about how to model policies or counterfactual choices. The following is a **practical proposal**, not a further theorem supplied by the source: define the decision variable at the level the agent can genuinely select, and include the selection mechanism in the probability model. Then evaluate the resulting action or policy using its conditional distribution. If changing the mechanism would change what a predictor can observe, that change belongs in the model too.\n\nRandomization illustrates why this matters. Suppose a predictor can read your decision procedure but cannot observe a fresh, independent coin toss. If you commit to one-box on heads and two-box on tails, learning the coin outcome after the predictor has acted need not tell you much about its earlier prediction. The probabilities appropriate to a deterministic one-boxing disposition cannot simply be copied over to the coin-controlled act. Conversely, if the predictor can forecast the coin or infer the procedure’s output, correlation may persist. “Randomize” is not a magical way to destroy dependencies; its effects depend on what is independent of what.\n\nThere is also a technical problem when \\(P(A\\mid K)=0\\). The ordinary ratio defining \\(P(O\\mid A,K)\\) is then undefined. This can arise if a fully specified model treats the agent as certain to take one action, while the agent is still trying to evaluate alternatives.\n\nPossible repairs include specifying conditional probabilities for the alternatives as part of a richer decision model, using carefully defined limits of positive-probability models, or evaluating feasible policies under an explicitly modeled selection process. None is automatic: different limiting models can produce different answers. A sound application should state which extension it uses rather than silently assigning a value to an undefined conditional.\n\nFinally, policy evaluation can raise dynamic-consistency questions. A policy preferred before receiving information need not recommend the action that EDT prefers after receiving it, because the later action may carry different evidence. Whether to commit in advance, revise later, or evaluate a whole sequence as one policy requires a specified sequential decision model. EDT’s one-step equation alone does not resolve every such case.\n\n## 8. Self-location and the limits of the source\n\nThe reference also mentions Sleeping Beauty, a problem in which an agent may be awakened at different times without knowing which awakening it is. Such cases introduce *self-locating uncertainty*: evidence concerns not only what happened in the world but where or when the observer is within it.\n\nEDT can be applied only after specifying the relevant credences, available bets, payment rules, and whether a choice is made once or at every awakening. The article notes arguments that evidential reasoning can help address Dutch-book objections to certain credences, while also reporting Conitzer’s argument that adopting EDT does not remove every such vulnerability. The safe conclusion is not that EDT settles Sleeping Beauty, but that decision theory and the treatment of repeated or self-locating choices interact there.\n\nThe article also mentions an “evidentialist’s wager”: even someone uncertain that EDT is correct might give its recommendations considerable weight when correlated choices greatly increase the stakes. That is a proposal about acting under uncertainty between decision theories, not a consequence of the basic EDT equation. How to compare theories, normalize their evaluations, and avoid double-counting remains a further question.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nEDT evaluates an act by the expected utility of outcomes *conditional on that act*. Its distinctive feature is that it counts an action’s evidential relationship to predictors, hidden states, and other agents, even when the action does not causally alter them. This explains both its appeal in Newcomb’s problem and the twin prisoner’s dilemma and the force of the objection that it can recommend managing appearances rather than producing benefits.\n\nTo use EDT responsibly, one must do more than insert numbers into a formula. One must specify the agent’s information, utilities, selection mechanism, and conditional dependencies—and recognize when those dependencies or the conditional probabilities themselves are unsettled.\n\n### References\n\n- Wikipedia contributors, [“Evidential decision theory,” revision 1373679688](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Evidential_decision_theory&oldid=1373679688), CC BY-SA 4.0.\n- Arif Ahmed, *Evidential Decision Theory*, Cambridge University Press, 2021.\n- Allan Gibbard and William L. Harper, *Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility*, 1976.\n- David Lewis, “Causal Decision Theory,” *Australasian Journal of Philosophy*, 1981.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABq8ObIc072A-TcZGuY6lNdOGORFWe76pFU6PfTJ5iJV6i2Zz9k5LrRN8lJ9dlgO9XYHO6LarXjuTEOKoUoDTMT0Ob65PkMxlNK8EcE06ocTWcEhHE7b47kY4ffmyXX6ZHeKOwCKGnN3ZMifRXKpETqYEjLTvmotr1jHIaQIvqbcXRPmk43jWsyDf5ErjkFnJ3T0pTpc2fln93kK9hjUgs2sBD4iuEw_BfWLHlb6AylM5URcHXPZA52yT_XxKpsEiq4oBU9sPmV6GrNdQyLYhZjl09ex7CveRcIMvmjSQrGswtA1oh5mMKyGnjAn6lMjHA4KP8xw9DAp7SlSiNsFSLPbLmjuFdVpXAdrqEvbbVo_zzpnDOb0dN_jCaKqOqw6gO5S4UIJYHNgfR3wvAhssr6c7e50-vgX9EAdd78y457qYf42AkZP6H3A7-iBQ2r4CYtVCUBYX-uBKpOCDgBPB3Ef6zsJW47TIBCMIbLh5dzUv3mnmV_PQL4UrgfBBcdRqXcrFtKV7YcIwZ2yqfWPp5VQWYnAGmebBsPcTTws4MQ7K0w7cPRP9GaLecUE0zZ4_3WLAvXoh4_KsubmKFPzWuebSwOnIIfbEMAmj0l_Za1N2yF7l_MkqeYmvGtvE3_RTZxupq6kx9VnEx1PdO2UdcTw0ojWuftjZkRwMK-Dc4hYQYrKiz4Ji05006axhLmU0ueYsqXuvnU09XJNlh63SOQLXqKAGXBpDnLvPysRMX_4bcNrdEesicjKOzpTi_6LQq4tuj2fhHMx5Q1gvO6hTaNkOhwUtp6flfpwRPT_yyjAUh3HwfYpmAIk_miqP2spXOgqjKc9goI_glyQmQD-evUdLTlb9Mx_76dW59Oxxh6NVj2nB4haQbs8OOjWnCzI5ZYD8rvf3hL_portXdjKzifcdZvu2maiNVTt1Ab